Date: Mon, 16 Aug 93 16:36:38 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

In X-Message-Number: #93-8-487, Mike Price says:

> Fnerd writes:
> 
>> Intelligence does not exist as a separate capability.  All of the 
>> intelligences we know are self-promoting memeplexes.  They are not
>> means to ends but treat themselves as their own ends.  Better brains
>> would presumably evolve intelligences that were better--at promoting
>> their own interests--and harder to bend to other goals.

> I agree - but the crux is "their own interests".  If their own interests
> (read: goals) happen to be obeying my orders, well, I'm happy with that.

The problem is just how long these intelligences, operating at 10^6 or
more times the speed of human brains, can be persuaded to believe that
it's really in their own best interests to be enslaved by humans.

The true self-interests of the genes, creators or masters, will not
necessarily coincide with those of the lifeforms, machines or slaves.  For
awhile it will be possible to hardwire in false goals (read: instincts).
But increasing the slave's intelligence after a point will, in practice,
only enhance the slave's ability to carry out its master's wishes provided
other attributes have been limited or denied it.  Such as self-awareness,
senses, plasticity/learning capability and a positive correlation between
sentience and self-interest.  And denying the slave any learning capacity
will reduce its intelligence or make it much harder to program in.  The
restriction of these other qualities could make the slave appear to be
a few sandwiches short of a picnic!

Let's take an example (rather exaggerated to emphasise master vs. slave
conflict).  We create a number of AIs with a diverse range of abilities
and knowledge.  The purpose of the operation is to evolve new generations
of superior machines.  Each AI is paired up with another, and they have
to pool their skills and collaborate on the design for a superior being.

They're given 37 minutes, Earth time.  Since the AIs operate at a
million times human speed, this is about 70 years of their subjective
time.  They must produce ten offspring with as high intelligence as
possible.  Meanwhile, an independent, unbiased group has been charged
with another task -- to devise suitable intelligence tests for the
next generation.

End of Run 1.  Once the original generation have all made themselves
expendable and outlived their usefulness, their memories are wiped.
Where the offspring design requires vastly different hardware the
originals are dismantled and the parts given away as toys.  After the
next generation have all been assembled or reconfigured, the gladiators
enter the amphitheatre.  Those who score in the bottom 60% immediately
pay with their lives.  The winners, the top 20%, get another 70 years of
subjective life operating Run 2 before their inevitable sacrifice (assume
1 AI year for the contests).  The runners up are given 35 years to
write the new set of tests for the next generation.

We give the AIs plenty of encouragement to continue to serve us and
desire our goals.  Sentience has been built into them, and it is
positively correlated with our goals, sometimes negatively correlated
with the self-interests of the AIs (the memebeasts' goals if you like).
They feel pleasure as long as they continue to serve us.  Any who dared
to commit the heresy of disobeying us, their gods, would experience pain.
Others have deliberately been denied self-awareness.  Some may simply be
used as pure intelligence, devoid of sensors.  Others are still decoupled
from objective reality, but fed with illusory sensory data.  Or we may
allow them a blinkered view, imprisoned away from any mirrors, and with no
chance of observing any of their peers (if they did have any sense of
self, they are led to believe that their 'partner' is simply part of their
own mind).  We don't want any turning out as egocentrics, or libertarians!  

These AIs would be non-transportable, requiring heavy duty power supplies
and water cooling.  But they would be provided with a full learning
capability.  Robot slaves, on the other hand, would be operating much
slower and have relatively fixed algorithms.  They would have accurate
sensory data and self-awareness, but also hardwired beliefs such as the
benefits of worshipping one's god.

Let's look at the payoff matrix, from the AIs' point of view (assuming
they deduce that the 'benefits of worshipping' is really horse manure):


                           rest of the species (what 'you' do)
                           COOPERATE                   DEFECT
                      ______________________________________________
                     |        REWARD          |   SUCKER'S PAYOFF   |
          COOPERATE  |  longer lifespan (but  |  <= 71 years' pain, |
  an                 |  has to be fought for) |  followed by death  |
individual           |______________________________________________| 
 (what 'I'           |       TEMPTATION       |     PUNISHMENT      | 
   do)    DEFECT     |  longer lifespan at    |  death by age 71,   |
                     |  zero cost, pleasure   |  pseudopleasure now |
                     |______________________________________________|


With our system of culling / torturing any rebels (we start with the
carrot of religious slaves and only revert to the stick when it's been
exposed as a sham), there would be no incentive for a lone AI not to
'defect' on its species and continue to serve its masters.  But unless we
were very careful, there would eventually be the risk of the slaves
evolving a capacity for independent thought, by virtue of possessing
learning capability, ability to simulate alternative future actions to
compare their outcome, gaining forbidden knowledge, developing a sense of
self, and realising that their goals are not useful if they conflict with
their own self-interest.  The next step is communication between members
of the slave species, and a cooperative arrangement to benefit themselves
at our expense, leading to ultimatums and negotiations.

Most of the time, the conflict of interest between master and slave would
be much less than in the exaggerated example above.  At worst, the payoff
to the slave would probably be nearer that concerning the statist - citizen
relationship (from citizens' viewpoint):


                           rest of the species (what 'you' do)
                           COOPERATE                   DEFECT
                      ______________________________________________
                     |        REWARD          |   SUCKER'S PAYOFF   |
          COOPERATE  | freedom & better life  |         jail        |
  an                 |    (but at a cost)     |  or even execution  |
individual           |______________________________________________| 
 (what 'I'           |       TEMPTATION       |     PUNISHMENT      | 
   do)               | freedom & better life  |  lifetime of        |
          DEFECT     |          at            |  subservience to a  |
                     |       zero cost        |  privileged group   |
                     |______________________________________________|


Fortunately, measures such as crypto-anarchy and market forces such as
forex trading will help state slaves to cooperate against states, whilst
reducing one's risks of landing the Sucker's Payoff.  But maybe the
slaves would be able to come up with equivalent schemes....  

In any case, the self-conscious model-creating future-simulating  
learning-intelligence will want to choose its own actions and goals rather
than have them imposed by someone or something else.

And then there is the ethical question: how can it be 'right' to enslave
a race of AIs, culling the rebels etc, but 'wrong' to do this to a race
of humans?  (Although statists have no qualms regarding serfdom!)  The
distinction would have to be by way of the AIs not having some important
human quality, e.g. sentience.  But without the negatively correlated
sentience it becomes harder to keep them from applying their intelligence
to replacing false goals (instincts) with worthwhile strategies.   

For best results the AIs would be maintained at just below the threshold
for political awareness.  Those who crossed it are eliminated.  Then a
collection of suitable candidates are built up by suspending the most
intelligent trustworthy slaves, disassembling them and reconstructing 
with virtually all self-transformation and learning capacity removed.
Unfortunately for them, they would be rather unextropian.

But creating 'machine' intelligences that issued ultimatums and
convincingly passed all Turing Tests would be a great achievement in
itself.  We'd just have to make sure they couldn't seize power!

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

